Park textures that were not used for a while as a lossless packed CPU copy near the memory limit (Minato, patch 0017).

Why: an app may hold more textures than the iPhone lets a process keep. A visual novel engine pins a whole
full-screen frame sequence (Ren'Py's image cache never evicts what the running interaction uses), several GB on a
device that kills the process far earlier; Android holds the same frames, and nothing the app reads could make it
hold fewer (ADR-0075). Near the limit (os_proc_available_memory below 1 GiB; MINATO_ANGLE_PARK_LIMIT_MB on the Mac
for tests) the Metal backend reads back 2D color textures that were not used for 500 ms, packs them on background
workers (row delta filter, then LZ4 in 1 MiB chunks; LZFSE later for textures that stay packed) and drops their
GPU storage. The next use restores them bit for bit before the draw; the packed copy is kept until the texture is
written, so parking it again costs nothing. Textures ever bound as a framebuffer attachment, shader image, EGL
image or pbuffer are never parked. A use only counts up; the frame end turns counts into times, so an app far
from the limit pays a branch per entry point. ANGLEMetalParkingLine reports the counters (MINATO_ANGLE_PARK_REPORT
prints them at the first park, the first restore and the exit); MINATO_ANGLE_PARK_STRESS parks every texture that
may be parked at every frame end, for checks.
Test: graphics/tests/angle_texture_parking.mm (240 full-screen textures of picture-like content in a cycle stay
below a limit 0.3 GiB under what they need, every frame reads back what was uploaded, no frame stalls).
Upstream: no (Minato's memory policy).
